R = []

R.append(dict(id="gp", n="Introduction to Geopolitics", k="GEOPOLITICS", w=2, c="#b8860b",
terms=[
 ["Geopolitics", "Jeopolitik", "The study of how geography affects politics and relations between countries.", "Coğrafyanın siyaseti ve ülkeler arasındaki ilişkileri nasıl etkilediğinin incelenmesi."],
 ["State actor", "Devlet aktörü", "A national government, political organization or leader that exercises authority over a country's security and resources.", "Bir ülkenin güvenliği ve kaynakları üzerinde yetki kullanan ulusal hükümet, siyasi kuruluş veya lider."],
 ["Non-state actor", "Devlet dışı aktör", "A participant in global affairs that is not a government, such as a company, a non-governmental organization or an individual.", "Şirket, sivil toplum kuruluşu veya birey gibi, hükümet olmayan küresel aktör."],
 ["National interest", "Ulusal çıkar", "A country's goals and ambitions, whether military, economic or cultural.", "Bir ülkenin askeri, ekonomik veya kültürel hedef ve amaçları."],
 ["Globalization", "Küreselleşme", "The process of interaction and integration among people, companies and governments worldwide.", "Dünya genelinde insanlar, şirketler ve hükümetler arasındaki etkileşim ve bütünleşme süreci."],
 ["Nationalism", "Milliyetçilik", "Promoting a country's own economic interests to the exclusion of others.", "Bir ülkenin kendi ekonomik çıkarlarını diğerlerini dışlayarak öne çıkarması."],
 ["Autarky", "Otarşi (kendine yeterlilik)", "A policy of national self-reliance with little external trade or finance.", "Dış ticaret ve finansın çok az olduğu, ulusal kendine yeterlilik politikası."],
 ["Hegemony", "Hegemonya", "A situation in which a country is open to trade but uses its dominance to influence others without cooperating on rules.", "Bir ülkenin ticarete açık olduğu ancak kurallarda iş birliği yapmadan üstünlüğünü başkalarını etkilemek için kullandığı durum."],
 ["Multilateralism", "Çok taraflılık", "Cooperation among many countries under shared rules, with open trade and capital flows.", "Açık ticaret ve sermaye akımlarıyla, ortak kurallar altında birçok ülke arasında iş birliği."],
 ["Bilateralism", "İki taraflılık", "Cooperation conducted between two countries at a time.", "Aynı anda iki ülke arasında yürütülen iş birliği."],
 ["Sanctions", "Yaptırımlar", "Restrictions on trade or finance imposed on a country, company or person to change behaviour.", "Davranışı değiştirmek için bir ülkeye, şirkete veya kişiye uygulanan ticari ya da mali kısıtlamalar."],
 ["Geopolitical risk", "Jeopolitik risk", "The risk that tensions or actions between actors disrupt the normal course of international relations and markets.", "Aktörler arasındaki gerilim veya eylemlerin uluslararası ilişkilerin ve piyasaların olağan işleyişini bozma riski."],
],
notes="""<h3>Actors and cooperation</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>State actors</b> are governments and their leaders. <b>Non-state actors</b> include companies, non-governmental organizations and influential individuals.</li>
<li>A country <b>cooperates</b> when it works with others on shared rules and standards. Its willingness depends on its national interest and on its resources.</li>
<li>Countries with few natural resources or with a need for trade routes have more reason to cooperate. Stable political institutions and long decision horizons also favour cooperation.</li>
<li><b>Standardization</b> of rules, such as accounting standards or shipping container sizes, makes cooperation easier.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Globalization and nationalism</h3>
<ul>
<li>Globalization is driven mainly by non-state actors seeking higher profits, access to resources and markets, and lower costs.</li>
<li>Costs of globalization: unequal gains, lower labour or environmental standards in some places, interdependence of supply chains.</li>
<li>Nationalism pursues a country's own economic interests, typically by limiting trade and capital flows.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Four archetypes</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th></th><th>Non-cooperative</th><th>Cooperative</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Nationalist</b></td><td><b>Autarky:</b> self-sufficiency, state control of strategic industries</td><td><b>Bilateralism:</b> cooperation with one partner at a time; includes regional blocs</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Globalist</b></td><td><b>Hegemony:</b> open to trade and capital but uses dominance to set terms</td><td><b>Multilateralism:</b> broad rule-based cooperation and open trade</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>Countries are not fixed in one box. They move over time and may behave differently in different fields.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Tools of geopolitics</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Cooperative examples</th><th>Non-cooperative examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>National security</td><td>Military alliances</td><td>Armed conflict, espionage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Economic</td><td>Free trade agreements, common markets</td><td>Nationalization, export restrictions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Financial</td><td>Free currency exchange, open investment</td><td>Sanctions, limits on currency exchange</td></tr>
</table></div>
<h3>Geopolitical risk</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Event risk:</b> tied to a date known in advance, such as an election or a referendum.</li>
<li><b>Exogenous risk:</b> unexpected, such as a sudden uprising or invasion.</li>
<li><b>Thematic risk:</b> a known risk that develops over a long time, such as climate change, migration or cyber threats.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Assessing a risk for a portfolio</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Likelihood:</b> how probable the risk is. Cooperative, globalized countries are generally less exposed to conflict but more exposed to contagion.</li>
<li><b>Velocity:</b> how quickly it affects markets. Exogenous shocks hit fast; thematic risks unfold slowly.</li>
<li><b>Size and nature of impact:</b> how large and how lasting. Impact is usually larger when the economy is already weak.</li>
<li>Many shocks cause only a short-lived flight to quality. Investors should separate temporary volatility from lasting changes in cash flows and discount rates.</li>
<li>Approaches range from scenario analysis and tracking signposts to a top-down view that sets country and sector weights.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Exam trap</b>Hegemony and multilateralism are both open to trade. The difference is cooperation: a hegemon sets the terms itself, a multilateral country follows shared rules.</div>""",
qs=[
 ["A multinational company that lobbies governments on trade rules is best described as a:", ["state actor.", "non-state actor.", "regional hegemon."], 1, "Companies, non-governmental organizations and individuals are non-state actors. State actors are governments and their leaders."],
 ["A country that pursues self-sufficiency and keeps strategic industries under state control, with little external trade, best fits the archetype of:", ["autarky.", "hegemony.", "bilateralism."], 0, "Autarky combines a nationalist stance with non-cooperation: the country aims to rely on itself."],
 ["A country that is open to trade and capital flows and takes part in rule-based cooperation with many partners best fits the archetype of:", ["hegemony.", "bilateralism.", "multilateralism."], 2, "Multilateralism is globalist and cooperative. A hegemon is also open to trade but uses its dominance to set the terms."],
 ["A scheduled national election whose outcome could change trade policy is best classified as:", ["event risk.", "thematic risk.", "exogenous risk."], 0, "Event risk is linked to a date known in advance. Exogenous risk is unexpected, and thematic risk develops over a long period."],
 ["The gradual effect of climate change on migration patterns and resource availability is best classified as:", ["event risk.", "thematic risk.", "exogenous risk."], 1, "Thematic risks are known and evolve over an extended period."],
 ["Economic sanctions imposed on another country are best described as:", ["a cooperative economic tool.", "a non-cooperative financial tool.", "a cooperative national security tool."], 1, "Sanctions restrict access to finance or exchange and are used against a counterparty, so they are a non-cooperative financial tool."],
 ["Which of the following is most likely a motive for companies to pursue globalization?", ["Protecting domestic industries", "Reducing dependence on foreign suppliers", "Gaining access to new markets and lower costs"], 2, "Globalization is driven by the search for higher profits through new markets, resources and lower costs. The other two are nationalist aims."],
 ["In assessing geopolitical risk, velocity refers to the:", ["probability that the risk occurs.", "speed with which the risk affects investments.", "size of the effect on portfolio value."], 1, "The three dimensions are likelihood, velocity and the size and nature of the impact. Velocity is how quickly the effect is felt."],
 ["A country is most likely to cooperate with others when it:", ["is self-sufficient in natural resources.", "depends on imports of essential resources.", "has frequent changes of political leadership."], 1, "Dependence on others for resources or trade routes raises the benefit of cooperation. Self-sufficiency and unstable institutions reduce it."],
 ["A free trade agreement between two countries is best described as:", ["a cooperative economic tool.", "a cooperative financial tool.", "a non-cooperative economic tool."], 0, "Trade agreements are economic tools used cooperatively. Financial tools concern currency exchange and investment."],
]))

R.append(dict(id="it", n="International Trade", k="TRADE", w=3, c="#2f8f5b",
terms=[
 ["Comparative advantage", "Karşılaştırmalı üstünlük", "The ability to produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country.", "Bir malı başka bir ülkeye göre daha düşük fırsat maliyetiyle üretebilme."],
 ["Tariff", "Gümrük vergisi (tarife)", "A tax on imported goods collected by the government of the importing country.", "İthal mallardan ithalatçı ülke hükümetinin aldığı vergi."],
 ["Quota", "Kota", "A limit on the quantity of a good that may be imported in a period.", "Bir dönemde ithal edilebilecek mal miktarına konan sınır."],
 ["Quota rent", "Kota rantı", "The extra profit earned by whoever holds the right to import under a quota.", "Kota kapsamında ithalat yapma hakkına sahip olanın elde ettiği ek kâr."],
 ["Voluntary export restraint (VER)", "Gönüllü ihracat kısıtlaması", "An agreement by an exporting country to limit its exports to another country.", "İhracatçı ülkenin başka bir ülkeye ihracatını sınırlamayı kabul ettiği anlaşma."],
 ["Export subsidy", "İhracat sübvansiyonu", "A payment by a government to domestic firms for each unit they export.", "Hükümetin yerli firmalara ihraç ettikleri her birim için yaptığı ödeme."],
 ["Deadweight loss", "Refah kaybı (ölü ağırlık kaybı)", "The loss of total surplus that results from a restriction or tax and is gained by no one.", "Bir kısıtlama veya vergi nedeniyle ortaya çıkan ve kimsenin kazanmadığı toplam fazla kaybı."],
 ["Free trade area", "Serbest ticaret bölgesi", "A group of countries that remove barriers to trade among themselves but keep their own policies toward non-members.", "Kendi aralarındaki ticaret engellerini kaldıran, ancak üye olmayanlara karşı kendi politikalarını sürdüren ülkeler grubu."],
 ["Customs union", "Gümrük birliği", "A free trade area whose members also apply common trade restrictions to non-members.", "Üyelerinin üye olmayanlara ortak ticaret kısıtlamaları da uyguladığı serbest ticaret bölgesi."],
 ["Common market", "Ortak pazar", "A customs union that also allows free movement of labour and capital among members.", "Üyeler arasında emek ve sermayenin serbest dolaşımına da izin veren gümrük birliği."],
 ["Trade creation", "Ticaret yaratıcı etki", "Replacement of high-cost domestic production by lower-cost imports from another member of a trading bloc.", "Yüksek maliyetli yerli üretimin yerini, ticaret bloğunun başka bir üyesinden yapılan düşük maliyetli ithalatın alması."],
 ["Trade diversion", "Ticaret saptırıcı etki", "Replacement of low-cost imports from non-members by higher-cost imports from members of a trading bloc.", "Üye olmayanlardan yapılan düşük maliyetli ithalatın yerini, blok üyelerinden yapılan daha yüksek maliyetli ithalatın alması."],
],
notes="""<h3>Benefits and costs of trade</h3>
<ul>
<li>Countries gain by specializing in goods in which they have a <b>comparative advantage</b> and trading for the rest.</li>
<li>Benefits: lower prices and more variety for consumers, economies of scale, more competition, more efficient use of resources.</li>
<li>Costs: firms and workers in import-competing industries lose out. The gains to the whole economy exceed these losses, but the losers are concentrated and visible.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Arguments for restricting trade</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stronger arguments: protecting infant industries until they reach scale, and national security.</li>
<li>Weaker arguments: protecting domestic jobs, protecting domestic industries from foreign competition, retaliation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Types of restriction</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th>Restriction</th><th>How it works</th><th>Who captures the gain</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tariff</td><td>Tax on imports</td><td>Importing government collects revenue</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quota</td><td>Limit on import quantity</td><td>Holder of the import licence; the importing government only if it sells the licences</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voluntary export restraint</td><td>Exporter agrees to limit quantity</td><td>Exporting country's firms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Export subsidy</td><td>Payment per unit exported</td><td>Domestic exporters, at taxpayers' expense</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum domestic content</td><td>Required share of local inputs</td><td>Domestic input suppliers</td></tr>
</table></div>
<h3>Effects of a tariff or quota in the importing country</h3>
<ul>
<li>Domestic price rises.</li>
<li>Domestic producers supply more and gain producer surplus.</li>
<li>Consumers buy less and lose consumer surplus.</li>
<li>Imports fall.</li>
<li>With a tariff, the government gains revenue. The remainder of the consumers' loss is a <b>deadweight loss</b>.</li>
<li>A quota has the same price and quantity effects as an equivalent tariff. The difference is who receives the amount that would have been tariff revenue.</li>
<li>A large importing country may push down the world price and improve its terms of trade; a small country cannot.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Capital restrictions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Governments may limit foreign ownership of domestic assets or residents' investment abroad.</li>
<li>Aims: keep strategic industries in domestic hands, protect the exchange rate, keep savings at home, reduce the volatility of short-term flows.</li>
<li>Cost: capital is allocated less efficiently and over time investors find ways around the rules.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Trading blocs, in order of integration</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Adds</th></tr>
<tr><td>Free trade area</td><td>No barriers to trade in goods and services among members</td></tr>
<tr><td>Customs union</td><td>Common trade policy toward non-members</td></tr>
<tr><td>Common market</td><td>Free movement of labour and capital</td></tr>
<tr><td>Economic union</td><td>Common institutions and coordinated economic policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monetary union</td><td>A single currency</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Trade creation</b> raises welfare; <b>trade diversion</b> lowers it. A bloc is beneficial when creation outweighs diversion.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Exam trap</b>With a voluntary export restraint, the extra profit goes to firms in the exporting country, which makes it the costliest restriction for the importing country.</div>""",
qs=[
 ["Compared with a tariff that reduces imports by the same amount, a quota differs mainly in that:", ["the domestic price does not rise.", "domestic producers do not benefit.", "the government may not receive the revenue."], 2, "Price and quantity effects are the same. Under a quota the amount that would have been tariff revenue goes to whoever holds the import licences."],
 ["Under a voluntary export restraint, the gain from the higher price in the importing country is captured mainly by:", ["consumers in the importing country.", "producers in the exporting country.", "the government of the importing country."], 1, "The exporter limits quantity and sells at the higher price, so its firms earn the rent."],
 ["When a small country imposes a tariff on an imported good, the most likely effect is:", ["a decrease in domestic production of the good.", "an increase in consumer surplus.", "a deadweight loss."], 2, "The tariff raises the domestic price, increases domestic production, lowers consumer surplus and creates a deadweight loss."],
 ["A group of countries removes all barriers to trade among its members and adopts a common set of trade restrictions toward non-members. This arrangement is best described as a:", ["customs union.", "common market.", "free trade area."], 0, "A customs union is a free trade area with a common external trade policy."],
 ["Compared with a customs union, a common market also provides for:", ["a single currency.", "common economic institutions.", "free movement of labour and capital."], 2, "A common market adds free movement of the factors of production. Common institutions come with an economic union and a single currency with a monetary union."],
 ["After a regional trading bloc is formed, members begin importing a good from another member in place of cheaper imports from a non-member. This is an example of:", ["trade creation.", "trade diversion.", "comparative advantage."], 1, "Trade diversion replaces a low-cost outside supplier with a higher-cost member and reduces welfare."],
 ["A country imports 200 units of a good after imposing a tariff of $5 per unit. Before the tariff it imported 300 units. Tariff revenue is closest to:", ["$500", "$1,000", "$1,500"], 1, "Revenue is collected only on units actually imported: 200 × $5 = $1,000."],
 ["Which of the following is generally considered one of the stronger arguments for trade restrictions?", ["Protecting domestic jobs", "Protecting an infant industry", "Retaliating against foreign tariffs"], 1, "Infant industry protection and national security are usually regarded as the more defensible arguments."],
 ["The costs of free trade fall mainly on:", ["consumers of imported goods.", "firms in export industries.", "workers in import-competing industries."], 2, "Consumers and exporters gain. Firms and workers that compete with imports bear the adjustment cost."],
 ["A government limits the amount residents may invest abroad. The most likely objective is to:", ["improve the efficiency of capital allocation.", "keep domestic savings available for domestic investment.", "increase the country's imports of consumer goods."], 1, "Capital restrictions aim to retain savings, protect the exchange rate or shield strategic industries, at the cost of less efficient capital allocation."],
]))

R.append(dict(id="fx", n="Capital Flows and the FX Market", k="FX MARKET", w=3, c="#c2417a",
terms=[
 ["Exchange rate", "Döviz kuru", "The price of one currency expressed in units of another.", "Bir para biriminin başka bir para birimi cinsinden fiyatı."],
 ["Base currency", "Baz para birimi", "The currency being priced; the denominator of a price/base quote.", "Fiyatlanan para birimi; fiyat/baz kotasyonunda payda."],
 ["Price currency", "Karşıt (fiyat) para birimi", "The currency in which the price of the base currency is expressed; the numerator of the quote.", "Baz para biriminin fiyatının ifade edildiği para birimi; kotasyonun payı."],
 ["Nominal exchange rate", "Nominal döviz kuru", "The quoted market exchange rate, not adjusted for price levels.", "Fiyat düzeyleri için düzeltilmemiş, piyasada kote edilen döviz kuru."],
 ["Real exchange rate", "Reel döviz kuru", "The nominal exchange rate adjusted for the relative price levels of the two countries.", "İki ülkenin göreli fiyat düzeylerine göre düzeltilmiş nominal döviz kuru."],
 ["Spot exchange rate", "Spot döviz kuru", "The exchange rate for immediate delivery, normally two business days after the trade.", "Normalde işlemden iki iş günü sonra yapılan anında teslim için döviz kuru."],
 ["Forward exchange rate", "Vadeli döviz kuru", "An exchange rate agreed today for an exchange of currencies at a future date.", "Para birimlerinin gelecekteki bir tarihte değişimi için bugün üzerinde anlaşılan kur."],
 ["Hedging", "Riskten korunma", "Taking a position that reduces an existing exposure to price changes.", "Fiyat değişimlerine karşı mevcut bir riski azaltan pozisyon almak."],
 ["Currency board", "Para kurulu", "A regime in which the domestic currency is fully backed by and convertible into a foreign currency at a fixed rate.", "Yerli paranın tamamen bir yabancı parayla karşılandığı ve sabit kurdan ona çevrilebildiği rejim."],
 ["Crawling peg", "Sürünen sabit kur", "A peg that is adjusted periodically, usually to offset inflation differences.", "Genellikle enflasyon farklarını telafi etmek için periyodik olarak ayarlanan sabit kur."],
 ["Managed float", "Yönetimli dalgalanma", "A regime in which the central bank intervenes to influence the exchange rate without a declared target.", "Merkez bankasının ilan edilmiş bir hedef olmadan kuru etkilemek için müdahale ettiği rejim."],
 ["Capital account", "Sermaye hesabı", "The part of the balance of payments that records flows of financial capital between a country and the rest of the world.", "Ödemeler dengesinin, bir ülke ile dünyanın geri kalanı arasındaki finansal sermaye akımlarını kaydeden bölümü."],
],
notes="""<h3>Reading a quote</h3>
<ul>
<li>A quote of 1.25 USD/EUR means one euro costs 1.25 dollars. The euro is the <b>base</b> currency and the dollar the <b>price</b> currency.</li>
<li>If the number rises, the base currency has appreciated and the price currency has depreciated.</li>
<li>The two percentage changes are not equal and opposite. If USD/EUR rises from 1.20 to 1.26, the euro has risen 5.0%, and the dollar has fallen by 1.20/1.26 − 1 = −4.76%.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Nominal and real exchange rates</h3>
<p class="f">real rate (price/base) = nominal rate × CPI<sub>base</sub> / CPI<sub>price</sub></p>
<p class="f">%Δ real ≈ %Δ nominal + inflation<sub>base</sub> − inflation<sub>price</sub></p>
<ul>
<li>The real rate measures the purchasing power of one country's residents over another country's goods. Trade flows respond to the real rate.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The foreign exchange market</h3>
<ul>
<li>The largest financial market, open around the clock, mostly over the counter.</li>
<li><b>Spot</b> transactions settle in two business days. <b>Forward</b> contracts fix a rate today for a later date. Swaps and options are also traded.</li>
<li><b>Sell side:</b> large multinational banks that act as dealers.</li>
<li><b>Buy side:</b> corporations, real money accounts (pension funds, insurers, mutual funds), leveraged accounts (hedge funds, proprietary traders), retail accounts, governments, central banks and sovereign wealth funds.</li>
<li><b>Hedging</b> reduces an existing exposure: an exporter that will receive foreign currency sells it forward. <b>Speculation</b> takes on exposure in search of profit.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Exchange rate regimes</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<tr><th>Regime</th><th>Key feature</th></tr>
<tr><td>Formal dollarization</td><td>The country uses another country's currency; no monetary policy of its own</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monetary union</td><td>Members share a single currency and one central bank</td></tr>
<tr><td>Currency board</td><td>Fixed rate with full foreign-currency backing of the monetary base</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conventional fixed peg</td><td>Rate held within ±1% of a target by intervention</td></tr>
<tr><td>Target zone</td><td>A fixed peg with wider bands, giving more policy room</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crawling peg</td><td>Peg adjusted periodically, passively for inflation or actively by announcement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crawling bands</td><td>The band around the peg widens over time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Managed float</td><td>Intervention guided by policy goals, no stated target</td></tr>
<tr><td>Independently floating</td><td>The market sets the rate; monetary policy is independent</td></tr>
</table></div>
<ul>
<li>The tighter the link to another currency, the less freedom the country has to set its own interest rates.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Trade balance and capital flows</h3>
<p class="f">X − M = (S − I) + (T − G)</p>
<ul>
<li>A trade deficit (X &lt; M) must be matched by a capital account surplus: the country borrows from abroad or sells assets to foreigners.</li>
<li>A trade deficit means domestic saving, private plus government, is too small to fund domestic investment.</li>
<li>Capital flows, not trade flows, are the main driver of exchange rates in the short and medium term.</li>
<li>Capital restrictions and currency intervention are used to reduce volatile inflows and outflows, with mixed success.</li>
</ul>
<div class="trap"><b>Exam trap</b>Always identify the base currency before answering. "The rate rose" tells you the base currency strengthened, whichever way the quote is written.</div>""",
qs=[
 ["In the exchange rate quote 1.25 USD/EUR, the base currency is the:", ["euro.", "US dollar.", "currency with the higher interest rate."], 0, "The quote gives the price of one euro in dollars, so the euro is the base currency and the dollar the price currency."],
 ["The USD/EUR exchange rate rises from 1.20 to 1.26. The US dollar has depreciated against the euro by approximately:", ["4.76%", "5.00%", "5.26%"], 0, "The dollar's value in euros goes from 1/1.20 to 1/1.26, a change of 1.20/1.26 − 1 = −4.76%. The euro has appreciated by 5.00%."],
 ["Over a year, the nominal USD/EUR exchange rate rises by 5%, inflation in the eurozone is 2% and inflation in the United States is 4%. The change in the real USD/EUR exchange rate is closest to:", ["−1.0%", "3.0%", "7.0%"], 1, "Change in real rate ≈ 5% + 2% (base currency inflation) − 4% (price currency inflation) = 3.0%."],
 ["An exchange rate regime in which the domestic monetary base is fully backed by reserves of a foreign currency at a fixed rate is a:", ["managed float.", "crawling peg.", "currency board."], 2, "A currency board commits to exchange domestic currency for the anchor currency at a fixed rate and holds reserves to back it fully."],
 ["A country that adopts formal dollarization:", ["keeps an independent monetary policy.", "gives up the ability to conduct its own monetary policy.", "must maintain a currency board to back its currency."], 1, "The country uses another country's currency, so it cannot set its own interest rates or money supply."],
 ["A regime in which a pegged exchange rate is adjusted periodically to offset differences in inflation is a:", ["target zone.", "crawling peg.", "conventional fixed peg."], 1, "A crawling peg moves the peg over time, often in line with the inflation differential."],
 ["A country that runs a trade deficit must also have:", ["a government budget deficit.", "a capital account surplus.", "a depreciating currency."], 1, "Imports in excess of exports must be financed by net capital inflows: borrowing from abroad or selling assets to foreigners."],
 ["According to the relationship between the trade balance, saving and investment, a trade deficit means that:", ["exports exceed imports.", "private saving exceeds domestic investment and the budget is in surplus.", "total domestic saving is less than domestic investment."], 2, "X − M = (S − I) + (T − G). A negative left side means private plus government saving falls short of investment."],
 ["In the foreign exchange market, the sell side consists mainly of:", ["central banks.", "large dealing banks.", "pension funds and insurers."], 1, "Large multinational banks act as dealers. Pension funds, insurers and central banks are buy-side participants."],
 ["A company expects to receive a payment in a foreign currency in three months and wants to hedge its exposure. It should:", ["buy the foreign currency forward.", "sell the foreign currency forward.", "buy the foreign currency in the spot market."], 1, "Selling forward fixes today the rate at which the future receipt will be converted, removing the exposure."],
]))

